  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1015 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nA cold front from the aforementioned low pressure system veers\nsurface winds to the northwest Tuesday morning, leaving the \nentire day with predominantly dry weather and noticeably cooler \n(but still unseasonably warm) temperatures. This front will then\nstall across northern IL / central IA, serving as a focal point\nfor redeveloping showers and thunderstorms to our south Tuesday\nnight as the LLJ cranks up south of it. The proximity of this\nboundary yields some 30-60% shower and storm chances in \nsouthern WI Tuesday night, mainly along and south of the I-94 \ncorridor, as well as the Hwy 18 corridor west of Madison. \nHighest chances are by the IL border. Predominantly dry weather \nis then expected through the day Wednesday as high pressure \nbuilds into the Great Lakes region and forces the frontal zone \nfurther south. \n\nA modest 500mb trough then digs south from south-central Canada\ninto the Great Plains Wednesday night into Thursday, with a\ndirectly-associated surface low pressure system (a clipper system)\ntracking east across southern Canada with it. The southern\ncomponent of this same trough axis phases with lingering oblong \nlow pressure / baroclinicity left behind by the aforementioned \ncold front, seeding the development of another low pressure system\nnear Missouri. The two low-pressure systems (and their \ncorresponding upper waves) would then track east and merge \ntogether east of the Great Lakes region later this week, \namplifying and intensifying rapidly. Many things need to phase \ntogether well in order for this picture to come together the way \nI've described it, but the gist of it is we could (potentially) \nsee some region-wide rainfall late Wednesday night through\nFriday morning as the southern low pressure system passes just \nsouth of the region. No organized thunderstorm potential is \nevident, but the rainfall is reaching a good general consensus \namongst model guidance (50-70% rain chances, which peak on \nThursday). \n\nStrong high pressure is expected to descend from Canada into the \nDakotas on Friday, working with the equally intense departing low \npressure to form a strong pressure gradient and very gusty NW \nwinds across WI on Friday. Strong CAA would then lead to high \nfrost potential (or perhaps even a freeze) this weekend. With \nsome of the more extreme guidance, overnight low temps into the \n20s could even be possible. On the other end of the model \nspectrum, there are far less amplified model solutions that depict\nmore of a double-barreled low pressure system as opposed to a \nstronger / unified low, as well as significantly weaker surface \nhigh pressure coming in from the west, and leave temperatures \nbelow normal but par for the course in mid Autumn. Until things \nbecome clearer, our forecast for this weekend will stick with the \nmiddle-ground solution of highs in the 50s and lows in the 30s. We\nwill keep you posted on trends. \n\nSheppard",
